<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "Complexity rises at emergence and then stops rising", finding_path("complexity-keeps-rising") %>
  on every substrate the programme has tested: an instruction cost, a structured world, a
  tape free to lengthen. The engine says why. A tape's only causal effect is whether its
  bytes land in a neighbour, mutational load grows with tape length while the copy benefit
  does not, and so a byte off the copy path costs its tape nothing. Junk is free, a
  good-enough copier inside a mutation cloud is the only attractor, and nothing in the world
  is scarce enough that one tape would gain from another tape's state rather than simply
  overwrite it.
</p>
<p>
  Sweep 9 prices that. Energy becomes a <strong>stock</strong>: every cell holds instruction
  energy that carries across epochs, so what a cell spends is gone until the influx pays it
  back and the world's total is bounded by cell count × cap. With the <span class="mono">$</span>
  op on, a tape can take what a neighbour saved, at a loss to the world. That gives one tape
  something to gain from another's state and the other something to defend — the arms race
  the only fitness-free result tying a mechanism to rising structural complexity rests on.
  The question this page answers: <strong>does complexity keep rising when energy is a
  contested stock?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The sweep crosses an economy bundle — <span class="mono">energy_influx</span> at 8 192,
  2 048 and 512 instructions per epoch, each with the steal op off and on at
  <span class="mono">steal_amount</span> 1 024, plus the economy off as a control — with the
  two room-to-grow caps whose plateau sweep 8 measured, <span class="mono">max_tape_len</span>
  128 and 256. Every arm ran 90 seeds, the control included, at 128×128 for
  20 000 epochs. Three arms were then extended to 270 seeds
  (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>, 2026-09-25):
  <span class="mono">1024×2048 128</span>, the one arm that read keeps rising at 90, and its
  control, to decide it, and the control at cap 256. The extension was chosen after the
  90-seed reading was seen, so the re-read of those arms at 270 is not a fresh test: it
  decides a reading that 90 seeds left on the fewest runs the rule reads. Every count below
  is of the runs that have finished. The hoard ceiling is 32 768 in every arm and
  <span class="mono">steal_loss</span> 0.5 throughout, so one steal delivers 512: a whole
  epoch's influx in the poorest arm, an eighth of one in the richest.
</p>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong> (DESIGN §1.3 sweep 9) and taken on emerged
  runs only — a crossing the replicator census or the copy rate confirmed, not every
  crossing the detector flagged. Per run, the median of the last decile of the post-crossing
  samples against the median of the first decile. A run counts as <em>measured</em> where
  the first decile of <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> medians above
  zero, so there is a ratio to take; a run carrying no
  <span class="mono">conserved_core_bytes</span> sample at all over that span is unmeasured,
  since the core clause cannot be read on it. That measured rule is a
  <strong>post-hoc amendment</strong> (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>,
  2026-09-21), made after this sweep's data were seen: as registered, the rule also wanted a
  nonzero first-decile median of the conserved core, and the Result below states what that
  rule read beside every reading taken under the amended one. A measured run
  <em>keeps rising</em> when <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> rises by
  at least 20% and <span class="mono">conserved_core_bytes</span> does not fall in bytes over
  the same span, so a core at zero at both ends has not fallen; it
  <em>plateaus</em> when the last decile sits within ±10% of the first. An arm keeps rising
  where at least half its measured runs do and plateaus where at least half plateau; an arm
  clearing both bars reads <em>mixed</em>, never rising, and one clearing neither, its runs
  having mostly fallen, reads <em>neither</em>.
  <span class="mono">dominant_compressed_len</span> is
  printed beside them and decides nothing: it saturates at the cap plus zlib's 11-byte
  envelope, which is what made the room-to-grow reading a measurement of tape length rather
  than of complexity. An arm reads at all on two measured emerged runs, or on a blank block
  of ten, which reads as an arm holding no replicator to read rather than as an arm still to
  be tested. And a steal arm whose <span class="mono">steal_rate</span> never leaves zero
  reads <em>theft never evolved</em> — never <em>theft does not help</em>, and never for an
  arm no <span class="mono">steal_rate</span> was sampled on, which reads unmeasured.
</p>

<% reading = @show.complexity_under_contest %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading.any? %>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_verdict", reading: reading %>
  <p>
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored runs at render time, and the
    per-arm reading is the one
    <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("host-parasite") %> and its transition
    report draw: an arm keeps rising only where at least half its measured runs do, on at
    least two of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    At 90 seeds <span class="mono">1024×2048 128</span> was the one priced arm that read
    keeps rising, on one of its two measured runs; read at 270 beside its control at 270, it
    is in the table below with the rest. That decides the arm the extension was run for, but
    not the sweep: <span class="mono">1024×8192 256</span> and
    <span class="mono">0×2048 256</span>, never extended, hold one measured run each, and it
    rises. One run is below the two an arm reads on, so the verdict above is unresolved and
    the finding stays partial rather than negative. Both rules read
    <span class="mono">conserved_core_bytes</span>, which compares aligned bytes, and a
    population of replicators that copy in reverse and their reverses reads zero there
    (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>, 2026-09-25), so a core at zero, which
    the amended rule counts as not falling, may be that confound rather than no core. The
    emerged runs of seeds 91–270 that finished after the orientation-aware
    <span class="mono">conserved_core_bytes_oriented</span> was sampled carry it too. Read
    with it in place of the aligned core, descriptively and on those runs only
    (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>, 2026-09-27), nothing moves: 14 emerged
    runs carry it, the oriented core reads zero at both ends of the span in 13 of them, and
    none raises <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> by 20%, so none rises
    under either core.
  </p>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_emergence", reading: reading %>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_theft", reading: reading %>
  <%= render "findings/complexity_arms_reading", reading: reading %>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("host-parasite") %> has reported a
    sample in this database, so there is no arm to read here. This page reads the arms at
    render time and states the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What would refute it</h2>
<p>
  A contested stock is the programme's bet that scarcity, not room, is what complexity was
  missing. It is refuted if the priced arms plateau where their own control arms plateau —
  same caps, same rate, same world, no economy — which would say a contested quantity buys
  nothing this substrate can spend on structure. The steal arms carry their own null: if
  <span class="mono">steal_rate</span> never leaves zero, theft never evolved and the arms
  say nothing about whether theft would have helped.
</p>
<% if reading.any? %>
  <p><strong><%= reading.refutation_sentence %></strong></p>
<% end %>
